@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-write-object-members-via-defineproperty-to-avoid-the-proto-accessor
+type: decision-log
+title: "Write object members via defineProperty to avoid the __proto__ accessor"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6901/6902 treat JSON object member names as opaque strings with no reserved or special name.
+`clone`, and `applyPatch`'s `add`/`replace` handling, previously wrote object members with plain
+bracket assignment (`container[token] = value`). JavaScript gives exactly one string key special
+runtime behaviour on plain assignment: `"__proto__"`, which (for object/null values) is routed through
+the legacy `Object.prototype.__proto__` accessor and re-points the object's prototype instead of
+creating an own data property. `Object.keys`/`JSON.stringify`/`hasOwnProperty` never see it as a
+member. Reads (`container[token]`, `hasOwnProperty`) were never affected — only writes.
+
+Consequences observed before the fix: `add`-ing a member literally named `"__proto__"` silently did
+nothing visible, and — more seriously — `clone` (which every `applyPatch` call runs on its input
+up front, even for an empty patch) silently dropped any pre-existing own member named `"__proto__"`
+on any document that had one (e.g. one produced by `JSON.parse`, which creates it as an ordinary own
+property, unlike assignment).
+
+## Decision
+
+Introduce a single `setMember` helper that writes via `Object.defineProperty(container, key, { value,
+writable: true, enumerable: true, configurable: true })` and route every object-member write
+(`clone`, `insertAtToken`, `replaceAtToken`) through it, instead of bracket assignment. This creates a
+real own, enumerable, writable, configurable data property for any key, including `"__proto__"`,
+matching how `JSON.parse` and object-literal computed properties already behave, and behaves exactly
+like assignment for every other key. `removeAtToken`'s `delete container[token]` needed no change:
+`delete` on an own configurable data property works uniformly regardless of key name; only assignment
+has the special-cased behaviour for `"__proto__"`. Array element writes (`container[index] = value`)
+also needed no change: numeric array indices don't collide with any inherited accessor.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `applyPatch`/`clone` now round-trip and patch documents that happen to have a member literally named
+  `"__proto__"` the same way they do for any other member name, with no silent data loss and no path
+  by which a crafted patch can repoint an in-memory object's actual prototype.
+- No new error subclasses or validation were introduced; this is purely a write-path fix. Patches that
+  were already rejected (or accepted) for other reasons are unaffected.
