@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+---
+id: task-003-audit-and-fix-applypatch-rfc-6902-conformance
+type: task
+title: "Audit and fix applyPatch RFC 6902 conformance"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` reported surprising results: some patches they considered invalid were accepted,
+some edge cases gave unexpected results, and failures didn't always match what the standard implies.
+Asked to review checking and application of patches and make the behaviour conform to RFC 6902 in every
+case, errors included.
+
+## What was checked
+
+Re-read RFC 6902 sections 3-5 and Appendix A, and re-verified `applyPatch`/`resolvePointer` against:
+
+- every worked example in RFC 6902 Appendix A (A.1-A.16: add/remove/replace/move/copy/test, nested
+  adds, ignoring unrecognized operation members, adding to a nonexistent target, `~`-escape ordering,
+  string-vs-number comparison, appending an array value) — all passed already;
+- the approved decisions in dl-002 (root-pointer semantics for add/replace/remove, move implemented as
+  remove-then-add with the `from`-not-a-proper-prefix-of-`path` check) — all still hold and are correct;
+- operation-shape validation (required `path`/`value`/`from` per op, unknown `op` values, non-object
+  operations, non-array patches) — all correctly rejected;
+- array-index syntax (`-`, leading zeros, negative numbers, out-of-bounds, one-past-the-end only where
+  `add` allows it) — all correctly handled.
+
+## Bug found and fixed
+
+`insertAtToken`, `replaceAtToken`, and the internal `clone` helper wrote object members with plain
+bracket assignment (`container[token] = value`). For the one member name `"__proto__"`, plain
+assignment does not create an own data property — JavaScript routes it through the legacy
+`Object.prototype.__proto__` accessor instead, which (for an object/null value) re-points the target's
+prototype rather than adding a member. Per RFC 6901/6902, a JSON object's member names are plain
+strings; `"__proto__"` is not special. The practical effect:
+
+- `applyPatch(doc, [{op: 'add', path: '/__proto__', value: {...}}])` silently did nothing observable:
+  no member was added, nothing serialized, and the result was handed back as if the operation were a
+  no-op — a "silent" wrong result, not a thrown error.
+- Worse, the same bug was in `clone`, which `applyPatch` runs on *every* input document up front: any
+  JSON document that legitimately has an own member named `"__proto__"` (e.g. produced by
+  `JSON.parse`, which — unlike plain assignment — creates it as a real own property) silently lost that
+  member on `applyPatch(doc, [])`, i.e. even with an empty, always-valid patch.
+
+Fixed by adding a `setMember` helper that uses `Object.defineProperty` to always create a real own,
+enumerable, writable, configurable data property regardless of key name, and routing the three mutation
+sites through it. Reads were already safe (`hasOwnProperty`/bracket-read on an existing own property
+both work correctly for any key name, `"__proto__"` included).
+
+Added two regression tests in `src/index.test.ts`: one confirms `add` can create a member literally
+named `"__proto__"` without altering the resulting object's actual prototype, the other confirms a
+pre-existing `"__proto__"` member survives an unrelated patch. Both fail against the pre-fix code and
+pass after the fix. Full test suite (56 tests) and `tsc --noEmit` pass.
+
+## Outcome
+
+One conformance bug found and fixed, with regression coverage; no other deviation from RFC 6902 found
+across the appendix examples, the normative operation semantics, or the previously approved
+root-pointer/move decisions in dl-002.
